<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("world_size") %>
<% sides = sweep.fetch(:param_grid).fetch("world_size").map { |arm| arm.fetch("width") } %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>

<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 2 sets two readings of emergence against each other, and they predict
  opposite things about a bigger world. If a replicator is a lottery win — one cell
  somewhere stumbles into a tape that copies itself, and the rest of the world only has to
  not destroy it — then cells are tickets: quadrupling the cell count should cut the
  expected time to the first transition by about the same factor, and the smallest worlds
  should mostly never transition inside the budget. If instead emergence is a
  <em>per-cell rate</em> — each cell needs its own local history of interactions before
  anything can replicate — then the transition epoch hardly moves with cell count, and the
  arms land on top of each other.
</p>
<p>
  The two readings differ in what a world is for. Under the lottery reading the world is a
  parallel search and its size is the search budget; under the rate reading the world is a
  population of independent local experiments, each running at its own speed.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Square tori of 64-byte tapes at
  <span class="mono"><%= sides.map { |side| "#{side}×#{side}" }.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span>
  — width and height travel together, so the arms are
  <%= sides.map { |side| number_with_delimiter(side * side) }.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %>
  cells —
  with <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %> seeds each and <%= epochs %> epochs per run. Every
  other parameter is at its default. The dependent variable is the transition epoch of
  DESIGN §1.2, the first sampled epoch at which
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> falls below 0.6 and stays below it for three
  more samples; a run that never crosses is censored, not zero.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  Open: the sweep is queued and no claim is made yet. The arms, the seeds that have
  finished and the transition epochs behind them are in the phase diagram and the runs
  table below, which read from the runs themselves — nothing on this page restates them.
</p>
<p>
  Two things will decide how a flat sweep may be read. A flat result is not readable as a
  per-cell rate until the
  <%= link_to "BFF positive control", finding_path("bff-control") %> transitions; before
  that, arms that all sit at the censoring line say only that the instrument is untested.
  And the <%= epochs %>-epoch budget is short next to the earliest transition any sweep of
  ours has seen — around epoch 5 000 — so a sweep in which nothing transitions anywhere is
  explained just as well by too small a budget as by the size of the world.
</p>
